scenario_questions.expected_answer:
A strong answer specifies: (1) debounce input to reduce requests, plus a last-request-wins guarantee using AbortController and effect cleanup; (2) an in-memory cache keyed by query to return instantly for repeats; (3) using `If-None-Match` with stored ETag and handling 304 to avoid payloads; (4) proper loading/error states and cleanup on unmount; (5) testability considerations.
Example structure:
```jsx
const cache = new Map(); // { q: { etag, data } }

useEffect(() => {
  if (!q.trim()) { setResults([]); return; }
  const ac = new AbortController();
  const t = setTimeout(async () => {
    // serve from cache immediately
    const cached = cache.get(q);
    if (cached) setResults(cached.data);
    const headers = cached?.etag ? { 'If-None-Match': cached.etag } : {};
    try {
      const r = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}` , { signal: ac.signal, headers });
      if (r.status === 304 && cached) return; // we already showed cached
      const data = await r.json();
      const etag = r.headers.get('ETag');
      cache.set(q, { etag, data });
      setResults(data);
    } catch (e) {
      if (e.name !== 'AbortError') setError('Network error');
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  }, 250);
  return () => { clearTimeout(t); ac.abort(); };
}, [q]);
```
Explain trade-offs: small memory cost for cache, debounce may delay first character responsiveness, ETag requires backend support. Tests: mock fetch and assert abort is called on rapid typing; assert cache hit path has no network.

Proposed flow: Use a fetch wrapper that always sets `credentials:'include'`. For unsafe methods, read `csrftoken` from a readable cookie and attach `X-CSRFToken`. On 401 from API (not from `/auth/refresh`), attempt one refresh by calling `/auth/refresh` with credentials. If refresh succeeds, retry the original request once. If it fails, redirect to `/login` and clear any UI user state. Use a reentrancy guard (e.g., a flag or queue) to avoid concurrent refresh storms. Never store tokens in JS. For protected routes, add a route guard that pings `/auth/me` on app load (with credentials) to hydrate user or redirect. Pseudocode:
```javascript
async function api(input, init={}) {
  const headers = new Headers(init.headers);
  if (!/^(GET|HEAD|OPTIONS|TRACE)$/.test(init.method || 'GET')) {
    const csrf = getCookie('csrftoken');
    headers.set('X-CSRFToken', csrf);
  }
  const doFetch = () => fetch(input, { ...init, headers, credentials: 'include' });
  let r = await doFetch();
  if (r.status !== 401 || input.toString().includes('/auth/refresh')) return r;
  if (api._refreshing) { await api._refreshing; return doFetch(); }
  api._refreshing = fetch('/auth/refresh', { credentials: 'include' })
    .then(x => { api._refreshing = null; if (!x.ok) throw new Error('refresh failed'); });
  try { await api._refreshing; } catch { return r; }
  return doFetch();
}
```
Routing: a ProtectedRoute checks hydrated auth state or triggers a silent `/auth/me` check; on failure, `navigate('/login', { replace: true })`. Avoid infinite loops by not attempting refresh for refresh endpoint itself, and by retrying at most once.

